[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: No scribe command loads a graph of its own any more

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-SCRIBE-COMMANDS-LOAD-IN-PROCESS
TITLE: No scribe command loads a graph of its own any more
DEPTH: implemented
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Every scribe command is a typed operation against the graph the daemon already
holds. The client parses its own command line -- it can, because the option
surface comes from the grammar FILE and needs no corpus -- and sends an
operation. The daemon validates that operation against the held graph and
never sees an argv. No command loads a graph of its own.

The last one that did was `new`, which reloaded mid-operation to resolve the
grammar alias on the file it had just written. That stopped on 2026-09-01:
[LINK: MECH-SCRIBE-NEW-BUILDS-THE-DOCUMENT].

What this node predicted got one thing right and one thing wrong, and the
wrong one is worth keeping. RIGHT: the commands could not move until their
verification was the workspace's rather than finish() -> verify()'s, which is
a change to the writer and not to transport. WRONG: the shape was expected to
be one socket call carrying the command line, and the seam is TYPED instead.
A daemon that parsed an argv would have to own the caller's working directory
and the caller's stdin, which is where `@file` and `-` are resolved.
<<<
